<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Přemyslid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 870–<a href="/years/1306">1306</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Přemyslids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Přemyslid dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> from the late 9th century until 1306, first as dukes and from 1198 as hereditary kings. Named after the legendary ploughman Přemysl, husband of Princess Libuše, it produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-i-duke-of-bohemia">Saint Wenceslas</a>, Ottokar II, who briefly ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and Wenceslaus II, who also became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The male line ended with the murder of Wenceslaus III in 1306.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Přemyslid dynasty" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wereszczyński · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COA_Premyslid.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Bohemia grew out of the Duchy of Bohemia, which the <b>Přemyslid dynasty</b> had ruled from Prague since the late ninth century, when Duke Bořivoj I accepted Christianity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After that state's collapse it passed, probably in the early eleventh century, under the <b>Přemyslid</b> dukes of Bohemia, who divided it into appanage principalities centred on Olomouc, Brno and Znojmo and granted them to junior members of the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Bořivoj I, the first historically attested <b>Přemyslid</b>, was baptised around 870, probably by Methodius, while under Great Moravian overlordship.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bohemia">Duchy of Bohemia</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke Bretislaus I of Bohemia arranged Moravia as a domain for junior members of the <b>Přemyslid dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-brno">Duchy of Brno</a></li><li><blockquote>Krnov (Jägerndorf) emerged from the <b>Přemyslid</b> lands of the Duchy of Opava.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-krnov">Duchy of Krnov</a></li><li><blockquote>The Opava <b>Přemyslids</b> ruled until the 15th century, after which the duchy passed to the Poděbrady family, the Hungarian king Matthias Corvinus and the Habsburgs.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-opava">Duchy of Opava</a></li></ul></section>
